<DocsLayout title="Limitations" description="Understand the current single-host boundaries before using MyPaaS.">
<section><p class="overline">Scope</p><h2>Single host by design</h2><p>MyPaaS runs its control plane and project workloads on one Linux host. The host remains the capacity and availability boundary, and the product targets an owner developer or a small trusted team rather than mutually hostile tenants.</p></section>
<section><h2>Current boundaries</h2><ul><li>Fresh supported Ubuntu/Debian hosts are Podman-first; Docker Engine is an explicit compatibility mode through the same Docker-compatible contract.</li><li>No Kubernetes, multi-node scheduling, or control-plane HA.</li><li>No multi-region high availability or automatic horizontal scaling.</li><li>Registry deployment currently targets public OCI images; private-registry credential management is not implemented.</li><li>No supported in-place Docker Engine → Podman state migration.</li><li>Engine-managed volumes may require separate handling before the built-in VM migration exporter can proceed.</li><li>Static recovery uses redeploy or roll-forward instead of container rollback.</li><li>Capacity depends on the application and the host resources available to it. MyPaaS does not claim a universal project count, user count, RPS limit, or application size for a given VM.</li></ul></section>
<section><h2>Resource responsibility</h2><p>On a single-host installation, builds, databases, the MyPaaS control plane, and running applications can compete for CPU, memory, storage, and network resources. Resource-intensive applications or builds may require a larger host or lower deployment concurrency.</p></section>
<section><h2>Security boundary</h2><p>Project workloads are restricted, but the MyPaaS API is privileged through container-engine authority. The current architecture is not a VM-grade or hostile multi-tenant isolation boundary.</p></section>
<section><h2>Operator responsibility</h2><p>Host sizing, provider availability, application architecture, backup verification, off-host recovery material, engine-managed storage portability, and infrastructure security remain the operator's responsibility.</p></section>
</DocsLayout>